[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was filed as one known issue: the parser reads dates month first.
Engineering closed it on 5 October. Read by cause, and with the desk's own notes, the reports merged
into it were separate problems. Each has a ticket of its own, and one of them is still open.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but the <code>one-day</code> problem is not fixed. Engineering's update covers
<code>parser</code> and <code>hint</code> only. Keep its ticket open.
</Callout>

## What was in #4471

<ProblemSplit ticket={4471} {issues} {status} list={['one-day']} />

Counts come from the ticket export at render time, the way the desk's cause table counts them.
Change the logic in `src/lib/kb/problems.ts`, not the numbers here.

## The problems

### `parser`: the shared parser read dates month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first. The shared parser read every date month first, so a
date whose day is 12 or less came out with day and month swapped: "Booked for 2 December. The
invite says 12 February." It reached every tenant, through the app, email and the booking-v2 form.
This is the problem #4471 was named for. **Fixed** on 5 October: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu
locales`.

### `hint`: booking-v3 told day-first tenants to type month first

booking-v3 printed `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People did what the hint said and typed the
month first; the form then read the date the tenant's way, day first. Only reports that came in
through the form of a tenant on booking-v3 have this cause. The parser fix does not touch it.
**Won't fix**: the booking-v3 form is retired, so no new reports can come in this way.

### `one-day`: the date moves by one day

These reports are not swaps. The invite lands a day early or late, or people in different places get
different days for the same meeting: "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday."
That points at time-zone conversion, not date order. The desk read them this way
(`desk-notes.json`). Its cause table counts them where they are filed, under `parser` by their
tenant, and so does the table above; they have their own ticket as well. **Still open**: the parser
fix does not move a date by a day, and engineering's update does not mention this problem.

## Why one ticket held them all

- [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports whose subjects match. Subjects
  describe the symptom ("Wrong day on the invite"), and every problem here has the same symptom.
  The same subject lines turn up under different problems.
- Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. The "intake tagged as" column shows it
  disagrees with the problem in every row; the tag is not a cause.
- The export repeats some reports across overlapping pages. Counting rows instead of ids
  overstates the ticket.

## What the desk does now

- `parser` and `hint` are closed with #4471. `one-day` stays open until someone fixes how invite
  times are converted between time zones.
- A new report about a date that moved by one day goes to the `one-day` ticket, not to #4471.
- Merging on subject alone put different causes in one ticket. Changing that rule needs a new
  decision record that replaces 0001; this page does not change 0001.

